                    @plan.Rules.O4,
                    @plan.Rules.O5,
                    @plan.Rules.O6,
                    @plan.Rules.O7,
                    @plan.Rules.O8
                    ]
        description
            Build @plan.Rules: app authors declare `invariants`, `turns` and `terminals` in `machine()`, every Story,
            generated run and sweep judges them, `flow check` proves terminals, and rule coverage reports what no run
            exercised. Terminals and `RuleSubjectUnreachable` extend @task.V3.T22's `flow check` analyzer as one more
            row, and `RuleBroken` is one more verdict kind in @task.V3.T114's run verdict.

            **Why now:** an app's guarantees are hand-written checks in single Stories today (NUMBER-12's bounds
            filter), which the generator and sweeps never judge. The design was settled with the user on 2026-11-19 and
            2026-11-20 and is the Plan; nothing in it is open but its Q1.

            **Read first:** @plan.Rules (every outcome; its goal lists what was not adopted and why), @plan.Testing
            (Stories, the generator, sweeps), @plan.Machines.O2 (fragments, tokens, `S.X.is`),
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/rules/rules.ts (Flow's run rules; `checked` is deleted by @task.V3.T114),
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/number-input/number-input.stress.test.ts (NUMBER-12); @spec.Types (R9,
            I12, I13; code:wiki/specs/v3/Types.md); @spec.Host (R11, R12, I14, I15, I21; code:wiki/specs/v3/Host.md).

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

            **Supersedes:** the `checked` helper and the `breaches`/`breachesOf` names in
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/rules/rules.ts (Host companion: rules return `Broken`; shared with
            @task.V3.T114, which the Spec makes the owner of the verdict and judge). Do not delete it before its
            replacement lands and the suite passes.

            **Risk:** the type surface: rule tokens need the machine's body type, which comes from @task.V3.T113, and
            shared bodies such as code:packages/flow-state-v3/fixtures/services/shop/machines/pay.ts#PayBody must still
            compile (@plan.Rules.O3); write the type tests first. Rules over `operations` depend on when a row turns
            busy (@plan.Rules.Q1). @plan.Rules.O7 needs traits and subroutines (@task.V3.T109) and the raise
            (@task.V3.T110); its `Story.fragment` part lands with @task.V3.T109, which builds `Story.fragment`.

            **Approach:** write the Spec from @plan.Rules with skill:to-spec, then its requirements as failing checks:
            type tests (@plan.Rules.V1), then the rules handed to @task.V3.T114's judge and the switch, rule coverage,
            terminals and `RuleSubjectUnreachable` in `flow check`, then `enabled` and `RuleBroken` outside tests.
            Rename the run rules' `breaches` and `breachesOf` to `broken` and `brokenOf` in the same change. Measure the
            suite with rules on and off before setting a budget.

            May change: `packages/flow-state-v3/src/`, `src/host/testing/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/` and a new Spec;
            @plan.Rules only through @plan.Pending, when the build shows it wrong.
        acceptance
            - A Spec written from @plan.Rules with skill:to-spec.
            - @plan.Rules.V1 to V7 pass; V8 passes for host Stories and for `Story.fragment` (built by @task.V3.T109).
            - Rule cost on the suite is measured with rules on and off and recorded in a comment on this Task.
            - `nub run check`, the suite and stress pass.
            - Correctness: a corresponding fixture app exists that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user
              would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the apps and how to build them); a Story tests that
              fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a test runs the Story; type tests show the
              feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect (two-sided pins, no annotations needed
              in user code); each new rule has a planted fault that `nub run faults` catches. See the existing tests
              (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. Tests cover @spec.Types (R9,
              I12, I13), @spec.Host (R11, R12, I14, I15, I21); the Invariants its tests check after every step are
              @spec.Types (I12, I13; tests T1, T4, T9), @spec.Host (I14, I15, I21; tests T1, T2, T8).
